  "theory": {
        "intro": "A constant function is a horizontal line, so its gradient is $0$ everywhere. A linear function $f(x)=mx+c$ has the same gradient $m$ at every point, so its derivative is $m$.",
        "facts": [
          "If $f(x)=c$ for a constant $c$, then $f'(x)=0$.",
          "If $f(x)=mx+c$, then $f'(x)=m$.",
          "For $f(x)=x$, the derivative is $f'(x)=1$.",
          "Rewrite a linear function as $mx+c$ first; for $f(x)=\\dfrac{ax+b}{d}$ the derivative is $\\dfrac{a}{d}$.",
          "The tangent to a straight line at any point is the line itself, so the instantaneous rate of change is $m$ everywhere."
        ]
      },
